<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-shears">
  <title>Distinguishing shearwaters</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "There are approximately ten thousand species of birds on the planet and no single individual has seen them
        all."
      </p>
      <p>--- Bernd Brunner</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p><em>Background.</em> Comparison of three species of shearwater.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How can the species be distinguished? If species labels were absent, what clusters might be
      found?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> <c>seabirds</c> data in the <c>CoModes</c> package.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> A list with a dataset of 153 birds and five variables, plus separate classification labels.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-shears-overview">
    <title>Distinguishing shearwaters and puffins</title>

    <p>
      The data contain morphological observations on Audubon's, Galapagos, and Tropical shearwaters. In several
      packages these birds are labelled as puffins, likely due to the genus name <em>Puffinus</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Prior publications used subsets of these data for clustering demonstrations, especially for the first two
      species.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-shears-two-species">
    <title>Comparing two shearwater species (Audubon and Galapagos)</title>

    <p>
      Categorical recoding and ordering support clearer comparison across the two-species subset.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-puffbar">
      <image source="images/puffBar-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Barcharts of five variables comparing two species, with Audubon's in brown and Galapagos in dark blue.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Eyebrows and collar are highly informative for separating species, undertail is mixed, and border contributes
      little discrimination.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-puffpcp1">
      <image source="images/puffpcp1-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Parallel coordinate plot for Audubon and Galapagos shearwaters using three descriptive variables.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Reversing undertail reduces crossings and highlights near-complete separation, including one unusual Galapagos
      case.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-puffdd">
      <image source="images/puffDD-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Comparison of Audubon and Galapagos shearwaters using collar bars faceted by eyebrows.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Collar and eyebrows together separate the species except for one Audubon case with no collar and pronounced
      eyebrows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-shears-cluster">
    <title>Can the species be clustered?</title>

    <p>
      In unsupervised clustering, species labels are not used. A faceted frequency display of variable combinations
      reveals dominant combinations that likely define cluster structure.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-puffmos">
      <image source="images/puffmos-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Barcharts of collar faceted by eyebrows, border, and undertail for Audubon and Galapagos shearwaters.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Most clustering methods should recover two groups close to species labels with only a small number of potential
      mismatches.
    </p>

    <p>
      Extending to all three species introduces stronger overlap between Audubon and Tropical shearwaters.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-puffmos3">
      <image source="images/puffmos3-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Barcharts of collar faceted by eyebrows, border, and undertail, coloured by species for all three species.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Galapagos remains partly separated, but overlap among the other species suggests three-way clustering is more
      difficult.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Audubon and Galapagos shearwaters can be almost completely distinguished by two morphological
        variables. Faceted barcharts indicate clustering should align closely with species for those two. With the
        Tropical species included, separation becomes harder.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How well can Galapagos shearwaters be separated from Tropical shearwaters alone?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-shears-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Matched frequency scales across related barcharts improve comparability.</p></li>
        <li><p>Category ordering and facet arrangement strongly influence readability.</p></li>
        <li><p>Faceted barcharts are effective for locating cases in multivariate categorical data.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
